  {
   "id": "de406a33-a33b-4745-b832-c50c534d089f",
   "term": "Bottom-up troubleshooting",
   "verdict": "NEEDS_REVISION",
   "suggestions": [
    {
     "field": "plain_english",
     "severity": "High",
     "issue": "The entry contradicts itself on which end is 'bottom', a direction that is the whole point of the method. plain_english defines bottom-up as starting 'at the source, such as the microphone or input, and works forward through the chain toward the output.' But scenario 1 ('first proving amps and speakers, then working back to the console') and scenario 2 ('from the physical layer (cabling) upward to application settings') both start at the OUTPUT/physical end and work back toward the source. A student who learns the stated direction and then reads the scenarios will be confused about how to actually run the method.",
     "suggestion": "Make the definition and scenarios agree. The scenarios follow the common convention (bottom = the output/physical end), so revise plain_english to: 'Bottom-up troubleshooting starts at the output/physical end of the system (e.g., the speakers and amps, or the cabling) and works back toward the source and higher-level settings' — or, if the source-first direction is intended, replace scenario 1 with one that begins at the mic/input and moves toward the speakers. Pick one direction and use it consistently across definition, plain_english, and scenarios."
    }
   ]
